# vehje-resolve — known gaps

Each section below keys a specific known gap in the shipped
crate, documented to the 50-word minimum so the mockspace lint
runner honours it as a suppression for the named type. Every
gap tracks back to a scheduler-integration task; none of them
is a design loss.

## Scope

`Scope::symbols` is backed by `std::collections::HashMap<Str,
Symbol>` in the skeleton compiler crate. The canonical target
is a scheduler-managed store: either a `Column<Symbol>` keyed
by a `Map<Str, SymbolSlot, N: Cap>` descriptor, or a single
`Column<(Str, Symbol)>` with sparse lookup, depending on how
`vehje-schedule` elects to partition resolver state. Tracked
to #131 (vehje-schedule as hilavitkutin WorkUnit home) and
#147 (validator framework single-walk visitor with scope
stack). Until the scheduler lands, the field stores names
inline so the resolver has somewhere to write.

## ScopeTree

`ScopeTree::scopes` is backed by a skeleton `Vec<Scope>` for
the same reason as `Scope::symbols`: the resolver needs
somewhere to hold scope state before the scheduler owns it. The
canonical target is a `Column<Scope>` indexed by `ScopeId`
(which is already a dense `u32` — so the column degenerates to
a contiguous arena). Tracked to #131. The skeleton's `Vec` is
not a design loss; it is the shape `Column<Scope>` will compile
to once `vehje-schedule` dispatches over it. Shallow
walk-in-place remains available throughout the transition.

## Resolved

`Resolved::resolution` is backed by a skeleton
`Vec<Maybe<Symbol>>` keyed by `NodeId.0` as dense-index. The
canonical target is a sidecar `Column<Maybe<Symbol>>`
materialised from a `hilavitkutin-persistence` cold-store entry
on incremental rebuilds, and reduced from an AccessSet walk on
fresh compiles. Tracked to #131 (scheduler handoff) and #134
(persistence spine cold-store shape audit, SUB-H1). Interim
skeleton holds the inline vector so `resolve()` can return
something other than `Maybe::Isnt`.

## Manifest

`Manifest::dependencies` is backed by a skeleton `Vec<Str>`
listing dependency crate names. The canonical target blends
three tasks: #166 (real TOML parsing of `Vehje.toml`), #131
(scheduler handoff — the manifest-walker becomes a WorkUnit),
and #134 (persistence spine — the crate graph is a cold-store
artifact). The skeleton `Vec<Str>` is the shape that all three
final homes reduce to at the leaf; it is not a design
compromise. Interim skeleton holds the inline vector until the
manifest parser and the scheduler/persistence plumbing land.
